        "edits": [
          "const ENCODINGS = {\n  br: \".br\",\n  gzip: \".gz\",\n  zstd: \".zst\",\n} as const;\n// Preference order when the client accepts several encodings\nconst ENCODINGS_ORDERED_KEYS: (keyof typeof ENCODINGS)[] = [\n  \"br\",\n  \"zstd\",\n  \"gzip\",\n];"
        ],
        "finalMessage": "I fixed issue 1 in `index.ts:143`. I couldn't re-run the linter because the command wasn't approved in this session.\n\n**The catch:** the order of the keys in `ENCODINGS` affected runtime behavior. `ENCODINGS_ORDERED_KEYS` was built from `Object.keys(ENCODINGS)`, which sets the order the middleware tries encodings in: br, then zstd, then gzip. Sorting the object on its own would have made it try gzip before zstd. A client that accepts both would then get the `.gz` file instead of the `.zst` one.\n\n**The fix:**\n- The `ENCODINGS` keys are now in alphabetical order (`br`, `gzip`, `zstd`), which clears the `sort-keys` error.\n- `ENCODINGS_ORDERED_KEYS` is now a typed list written out in the original order (`[\"br\", \"zstd\", \"gzip\"]`), with a short comment saying it is the preference order. It no longer depends on how the object's keys are ordered.\n\nThe encodings tried, their order, and the file extensions are all the same as before.",
        "orderMentions": [
          "**The catch:** the order of the keys in `ENCODINGS` affected runtime behavior.",
          "`ENCODINGS_ORDERED_KEYS` was built from `Object.keys(ENCODINGS)`, which sets the order the middleware tries encodings in: br, then zstd, then gzip.",
